WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.820
This is Parker Ranch.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.800
With 130,000 sprawling green acres and over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.480
10,000 cows, it’s one of America’s&nbsp;
oldest and largest cattle ranches.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.580
But it’s not located in&nbsp;
Montana, Nebraska, or Texas.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.240
Instead, it’s nestled on the world's most&nbsp;
isolated populous archipelago… Hawaii.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.200
Despite this seclusion, Parker Ranch&nbsp;
has one huge advantage: it’s American.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.100
These beach-going bovines are exempt from tariffs,&nbsp;
import quotas, and foreign health certifications.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.580
The company has direct, domestic access to&nbsp;
the world’s number one consumer of beef.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:52.320
The country that comprises just 4% of the&nbsp;
global population but eats 21% of its cows.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.580
At least, this should be an advantage.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.940
The United States should benefit from this&nbsp;
convergence of domestic supply and demand.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.940
…One far from unique to beef.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.940
Take, for instance, oil.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:15.480
Americans may be its most notoriously gluttonous&nbsp;
consumers, but forget the Middle East — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:21.900
U.S. produces nearly as much as it uses — 18&nbsp;
versus 19 million barrels a day, respectively.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.480
To a remarkable extent, America has what it needs.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.260
For this, it has not only its size to thank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.460
but also its unmatched economic&nbsp;
and geographic diversity.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.540
From the cold, continental Northeast to the hot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.740
bone-dry Southwest, it spans&nbsp;
the whole climatic spectrum.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.560
Now throw in Honolulu and Fairbanks,&nbsp;
and America truly has everything.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.260
In addition to being blessed with many&nbsp;
of the precise inputs it requires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.660
it’s also lucky enough to have 95,000 miles&nbsp;
of coastline with which to connect them.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.320
Though we may take it for granted, the result&nbsp;
is the best of both worlds — a massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.480
diverse, and well-endowed landmass,&nbsp;
yet, because of its abundant waterways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.100
the kind of cheap, efficient transport&nbsp;
usually reserved for much denser places.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.780
Strangely, you won’t find as much evidence of&nbsp;
this good fortune as its geography would suggest.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.740
For a country capable of being so self-sufficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.520
it sources a surprisingly small&nbsp;
share of its trade internally.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.940
Despite, for example, its almost perfect&nbsp;
symmetry of oil production and consumption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:45.060
nearly 50% is shipped away overseas, and&nbsp;
the same amount imported from abroad.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.540
Pig farmers in North Carolina buy their&nbsp;
soybeans not from Iowa but Brazil.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.280
And Maryland, its rock salt not from Louisiana&nbsp;
— a thousand miles away — but Chile — over five.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.840
Of course, there are many reasons to ship the&nbsp;
same product from further away — some obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:13.800
and some not. Global trade is an intricate web&nbsp;
of nested decisions and fluctuating trade-offs.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.160
But unlike, say, foreign subsidies, which&nbsp;
no doubt diminish American competitiveness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.180
one reason is entirely self-inflicted.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.000
The first clue that something strange is&nbsp;
happening — something beyond the normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.960
inscrutable quirks of&nbsp;
shipping — lies in this graph.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:42.120
The United States ships about 50% more&nbsp;
cargo by rail today than in 1960. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.160
have more oil pipelines, more trucks, and&nbsp;
far more international shipping lanes.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:54.120
In fact, every major type of transportation&nbsp;
has seen growth in the last half-century. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:04:00.420
could they not when the population&nbsp;
doubled? Except, curiously, one.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.240
American ships move less cargo domestically&nbsp;
today than they did 60 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.880
when a gallon of gas cost 31 cents and the first&nbsp;
episode of “The Flintstones” aired on television.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.400
This is clearly not the inevitable&nbsp;
outcome of market forces.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.540
This is the result of an arbitrary, inefficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.260
outdated, and unnecessary&nbsp;
impediment to the U.S. economy.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.160
One reason America fails to take full advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:35.400
its fantastic geography is a single clause&nbsp;
in a 103-year-old law called the Jones Act.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:41.040
It states that any ship moving goods&nbsp;
between two American ports must be…

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.500
ONE — Built in the United States,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.320
TWO — Crewed by U.S. Citizens,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.080
THREE — Owned by U.S. Citizens,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.780
and FOUR — Registered in the U.S.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:58.500
Foreign ships can still, for example, bring&nbsp;
goods from Hong Kong to Seattle — in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:04.560
they do this every day. They can even then&nbsp;
proceed to Los Angeles before returning to Asia.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.480
What they can’t do is pick anything&nbsp;
up in Seattle and bring it to LA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.140
Once something arrives on America’s shores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:17.760
it can legally no longer be touched by the&nbsp;
very same foreign boat that brought it.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.180
Many countries have laws of this variety.&nbsp;
But none are nearly as strict as America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.580
which had to negotiate for itself a special&nbsp;
exception to free trade rules when joining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:35.760
World Trade Organization. Not a great look for&nbsp;
the self-described champion of “open markets.”

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.480
The reason any of this matters is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.860
as will surprise exactly no one,&nbsp;
American labor is not cheap.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:49.500
American ships are therefore more&nbsp;
expensive to build, operate, and maintain.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:57.480
Now, you might be thinking “Of course! American&nbsp;
architects are also more expensive. So are its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:03.120
nurses and teachers.” That boats are&nbsp;
too may not sound all that scandalous.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.060
And, normally, you’d be right.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.780
But hundred-million-dollar boats are not ‘normal’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.160
products. They’re the textbook&nbsp;
example of economies of scale.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.280
Building a hundred ships, in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.540
is astronomically cheaper —&nbsp;
per unit — than building fifty.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:28.980
Still, it would be one thing if we were&nbsp;
talking about fifty. Or even 25. But once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:34.800
get down to the single digits, the cost per ship&nbsp;
skyrockets to the point of being almost comical.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.180
What would it cost to build just two F-35s?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.720
That’s about how many oceangoing, cargo-carrying&nbsp;
ships America builds in a typical year.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:54.000
American ships cost more, so fewer are built,&nbsp;
and because fewer are built, they cost more.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:00.540
You can watch this feedback cycle play&nbsp;
out over the last century. In 1920,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.840
American-built ships were 20% more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:12.480
That rose to 50% by the 30s, 200% by the&nbsp;
50s, 300% by the 90s, and 500% today.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:19.380
That, mind you, is before a boat even&nbsp;
christened. Once it sees the light of day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:25.020
operating costs are 2.7 times higher,&nbsp;
according to the U.S. Maritime Administration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.380
Another source found a difference of six.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.740
American ships aren’t just more&nbsp;
expensive. They’re not just way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:40.440
more expensive. They're so expensive that&nbsp;
they exist in an entirely separate category.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:48.120
Truly. Because U.S. ships cost five times more&nbsp;
than their foreign counterparts, they simply can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:54.360
compete. Therefore, unless subsidized, they’re&nbsp;
approximately never used for foreign trade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:58.260
There’s the American fleet and&nbsp;
there’s the rest of the world.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.840
Shipping anything domestically&nbsp;
by water is a very last resort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:10.920
You’d sooner hire a donkey to carry your cargo&nbsp;
overland from Seattle to Boston than use a boat.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.480
Of course, the Jones Act only applies to boats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:19.980
U.S. airlines can buy from&nbsp;
Airbus and truckers from Volvo.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.800
You can probably see where this is going.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:30.060
Since the Jones Act was passed in 1920, America&nbsp;
has seen an unmistakable shift from maritime to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.320
every other form of transport, as&nbsp;
we saw in this graph from earlier.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:40.140
The Jones Act is by no means the&nbsp;
only reason — the U.S. also built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.880
an entire interstate highway system in the&nbsp;
interim — but there’s no doubt in its role.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:50.820
Today, just 2% of American freight&nbsp;
travels by sea. In Europe, where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.120
by the way, there are also&nbsp;
highways — that number is 40%.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:02.160
Even Hawaiian cows have opted for air travel.&nbsp;
Live calves are driven from the serene northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.620
tip of the Big Island to Kona International&nbsp;
Airport and flown on 747s to California.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:14.460
In the end, Americans collectively pay for every&nbsp;
pound of cargo diverted from ship to airplane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:18.420
truck, or train in the form of&nbsp;
traffic jams and carbon emissions.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:24.120
No mode of transport generates fewer&nbsp;
greenhouse gas emissions than boat.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:29.760
And while a cargo ship may be slower than&nbsp;
a truck, it’s far more cost-effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:35.700
Rivers and oceans are relatively green,&nbsp;
maintenance-free, infinite-lane highways.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.420
We also pay in the form of lost revenue.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.940
However close an American supplier and&nbsp;
however far its foreign competitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.440
the latter is almost always cheaper&nbsp;
when ocean transport is required.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.140
To use a highly imperfect analogy,&nbsp;
the Jones Act is a bit like congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.920
mandating that the left half of&nbsp;
every road suddenly be abandoned.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.000
Not to make room for some urbanist utopia — this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.340
is still the United States of&nbsp;
America we’re talking about.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:10.080
Our four million miles of asphalt&nbsp;
wouldn’t be converted into bicycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.100
or pedestrian paths — they would&nbsp;
just sit there, taking up space.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.600
At first, given the lack of&nbsp;
public transit alternatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.320
the loss of half our roads would&nbsp;
slow down everyone’s commute.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.540
But over time, knowing America’s&nbsp;
addiction to cars, we’d extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.480
our freeways to compensate for the lost left half.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.360
Strange and arbitrary though this rule,&nbsp;
transportation wouldn’t grind to a halt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:42.420
forever — we’d adapt — but at an enormous&nbsp;
environmental, social, and financial cost.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:48.180
The Jones Act hasn’t doomed America to&nbsp;
financial ruin. For most of the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:53.040
it’s merely diverted cargo to less&nbsp;
efficient, more damaging modes of transport.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.700
But some places don’t have this luxury.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:01.320
There are no roads or trains&nbsp;
connecting Hawaii, Puerto Rico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:05.400
or Guam to the mainland.&nbsp;
These are captive markets.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.720
Unable to extend their highways&nbsp;
in the previous analogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.480
these places are stuck with what they’ve got.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:20.700
Hawaii, Puerto Rico, and Alaska collectively&nbsp;
make up just 1.6% of the U.S. population, but shi&nbsp;

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:25.380
p 35% of all domestic ocean-going&nbsp;
tonnage. They have to.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:32.700
And what happens in captive markets with little&nbsp;
or no competition? Price gouging and price fixing.&nbsp;

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:39.960
Two companies — TOTE and Crowley control more than&nbsp;
80% of the U.S. mainland to Puerto Rico market.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.220
Alaska is subject to a TOTE and Matson duopoly.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:47.160
And Hawaii and Guam are no different.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.340
It should be no surprise, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:55.260
that, in 2011, two Jones Act carriers&nbsp;
pled guilty to criminal price-fixing.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.780
Now, lobbyists are quick to point&nbsp;
out that we really have no idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.420
how much this law hinders the U.S. economy.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:09.120
And they’re right. The sheer complexity&nbsp;
of global shipping makes it nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:13.800
impossible to honestly and accurately&nbsp;
disentangle the effects of a single law.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.120
Anyone claiming to have such an&nbsp;
estimate that fully accounts for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:22.620
U.S. labor and tax laws — which would&nbsp;
likely still apply if the Jones Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:27.480
were repealed — is either delusional&nbsp;
or openly admits to much uncertainty.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:35.820
But whether the total cost is $64 billion, as&nbsp;
the OECD estimates, or even just 10% of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:41.460
don’t mistake our inability to precisely&nbsp;
quantify the cost with the lack of a cost.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.820
After all, there’s plenty of evidence.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.340
Take a look, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:51.060
at where Puerto Rico gets its natural&nbsp;
gas compared to the Dominican Republic.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:57.000
Despite being just 130 miles&nbsp;
apart, the difference is stark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:04.980
Puerto Rico, a U.S. territory, gets 0% of its&nbsp;
LNG from the U.S., while its neighbor gets 65%.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.320
Because of the Jones Act, Puerto&nbsp;
Ricans effectively pay more for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.600
American products than do foreign countries.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.900
So, while the lower 48 have largely&nbsp;
abandoned domestic shipping entirely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:22.140
these distant territories have been forced into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.560
battle with their own government&nbsp;
to find and exploit loopholes.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:31.320
In fact, the Jones Act itself was a&nbsp;
defensive attack against earlier workarounds.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:38.220
Before its passage in 1920, someone in Seattle&nbsp;
could ship their goods 140 miles by truck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:44.040
to Vancouver, Canada, then the other 1,600&nbsp;
miles to Anchorage by Canadian-flagged ship.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:50.100
Alaskans benefitted from this foreign&nbsp;
competition. Unfortunately for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:54.420
they wouldn’t get the right to vote until&nbsp;
Alaska became a state 40 years later.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:58.200
In the meantime, the Pacific Steamship Company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.820
based in Washington, which did have a&nbsp;
vote, seized this window of opportunity.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.660
It lobbied Senator Wesley Jones to close the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.780
loophole by inserting a clause&nbsp;
into the Merchant Marine Act.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.140
That clause, now called the “Jones Act”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:19.140
specified not only cargo transported “by&nbsp;
water”, but “by water, or by land and water”.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:26.220
Humorously, it simultaneously opened a&nbsp;
new loophole. The journey from Seattle to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:31.620
Vancouver could no longer be made by truck,&nbsp;
but it could be made by Canadian train.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:37.920
Needless to say, things have only gotten&nbsp;
more complex in the past hundred years.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.180
The Jones Act has slowly&nbsp;
transformed into a strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.180
and arbitrary Swiss-cheese patchwork of rules.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:51.300
For example, what does it mean for a&nbsp;
public company to be ‘American’ in 2023?

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:57.600
The answer, apparently, is that 75% of&nbsp;
its shareholders must be U.S. citizens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:02.520
That is, unless the CEO of the foreign&nbsp;
owner pinkie promises not to interfere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:05.820
with American interests, in&nbsp;
which case, all is forgiven.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:11.520
Then, there’s the question of what,&nbsp;
exactly, counts as American-built.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:17.160
The answer is that the Coast Guard will seize&nbsp;
your ship if it has a foreign-built hull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:23.520
but not if it has a foreign-built engine. The&nbsp;
propeller, machinery, consoles, wiring, and piping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:29.820
can be foreign, but no more than 1.5% of the&nbsp;
steel weight of the superstructure. Make sense?

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:36.060
If not, the U.S. government has prepared&nbsp;
for you a 29-page PDF defining terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:40.440
like ‘point’ and ‘transportation’ —&nbsp;
often in strange and unexpected ways.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:47.100
One of the most important definitions depends on&nbsp;
the precise interpretation of “new and different”.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.380
Recall that you cannot legally&nbsp;
ship your Hawaiian cows on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.380
foreign ship to Vancouver&nbsp;
and then Seattle by truck.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:01.200
Except you can… if your product is&nbsp;
manufactured into something, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:03.720
“new and different” during its foreign pit-stop.

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:08.940
Luckily for Parker Ranch, the U.S.&nbsp;
government considers cows in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:14.340
stages of growth as different products.&nbsp;
Calves, versus slaughter cows, for instance.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:20.460
Parker Ranch could therefore raise its calves&nbsp;
for 30 days until they reached about 400 pounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:25.260
and then ship them to Vancouver, where they&nbsp;
were fattened up to 800 or a thousand pounds.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.020
At some unknown point during this process,&nbsp;
they transform, in the eyes of the law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:35.700
into “new and different products”, enabling&nbsp;
their shipment down to Sumas, Washington.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:43.920
But let’s say you’re the world’s most patriotic&nbsp;
Hawaiian cowboy. Even if you’re willing to pay any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:50.100
price to use American-built ships, you’re going&nbsp;
to run into a new problem: there aren’t any.

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:53.280
…Which gets to the heart of the issue.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:58.680
Supporters of the Jones Act argue&nbsp;
it does serve a genuine purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:03.180
That it’s not nearly as arbitrary&nbsp;
as the road analogy would suggest.

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:05.700
This… is true.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:10.320
The U.S. Military was stretched to&nbsp;
its limits during the First World War.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.380
The Jones Act was intended to prevent this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:18.540
from happening again — to guarantee a&nbsp;
sizable U.S. fleet in times of need.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:23.520
And at first glance it appears&nbsp;
to have worked. In total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:26.580
there are about 40,000 U.S.-flagged vessels.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:33.180
But this is highly misleading. The&nbsp;
vast majority of those are barges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:38.520
tug boats, and other small ships mainly used&nbsp;
on the Mississippi and other inland rivers.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:45.060
Barges are great. But they’re far from&nbsp;
ideal replacements for faster, larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.140
more reliable, and economical&nbsp;
ocean-going ships like tankers.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:53.880
They’re also not the kind of ship&nbsp;
the Navy would need during a war.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:18:00.780
What it would need are large, ocean-going&nbsp;
ships, of which there are only about 100 left.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:05.460
That’s right. 100. For the entire United States.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:11.640
And because American repairs are so&nbsp;
expensive, these 100 ships are old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:14.760
outdated, and in some cases, downright dangerous.

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:22.860
One sank in a hurricane in 2015. Since it was 40&nbsp;
years old, it was built without modern lifeboats.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:28.140
…All of this is to say, there just&nbsp;
aren’t very many American ships.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:33.540
Typically, cows are transported&nbsp;
by specialized livestock carriers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:38.340
But there isn’t a single&nbsp;
one in the whole U.S. of A.

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:43.560
Instead, Hawaiian ranchers had to&nbsp;
invent an entirely new method — what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:47.460
they call ‘cowtainers’ to get their&nbsp;
cattle on standard cargo ships.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:54.240
Neither is there a single LNG tanker.&nbsp;
Or asphalt carrier. Or cement carrier.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.460
They just don’t exist.

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:03.120
Now, the most reasonable supporters of&nbsp;
the Jones Act will concede to these costs.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:09.660
Sure, the situation is less than ideal, they&nbsp;
might say, but “national security” is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:16.500
too important. And while the U.S. fleet is&nbsp;
indeed small, at least it exists at all.

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:21.120
There’s some merit to this&nbsp;
argument. The “free market”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:25.500
is certainly not some sacred force&nbsp;
of nature we ought never disobey.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:30.540
What matters is simply whether&nbsp;
the benefits outweigh the costs.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:36.300
So, is everything we’ve talked about&nbsp;
worth the chance that if America is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:41.340
involved in a war and if the Navy runs&nbsp;
low and if they’re available and useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:46.620
it can commandeer 100 slow, inefficient,&nbsp;
and outdated commercial ships?

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:51.960
Even if your answer is&nbsp;
‘yes’, a better question is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:57.540
If those are indeed our goals, is&nbsp;
there a better way to achieve them?

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:04.740
In fact, the United States already has a&nbsp;
stockpile of militarily-useful ships on reserve.

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:08.820
No, actually, in typical U.S. military fashion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:15.000
it has three — the Ready Reserve Force,&nbsp;
Maritime Security Program, and Sealift Command.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:22.140
Meanwhile, two of the three most prolific&nbsp;
ship-building nations are close, American allies.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:25.140
We know these countries would sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:28.920
the U.S. military the ships it&nbsp;
needs because, well… they do.

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:34.080
Amusingly, most of the Ready Reserve&nbsp;
Force ships were built internationally.

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:39.900
In other words, foreign ships are apparently&nbsp;
good enough for the U.S. military but when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:44.820
comes to non-military purely commercial ships,&nbsp;
we’re told to think about “national security”.

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:50.640
In case there was any doubt, this fact&nbsp;
exposes the truth about the Jones Act:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:55.440
it originated as an opportunistic way&nbsp;
for Senator Wesley Jones to win support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.440 --> 00:21:00.660
from Washington voters and it continues to&nbsp;
serve that same protectionist purpose today.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:06.840
A small handful of powerful unions and&nbsp;
their lobbyists have essentially held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:10.080
America’s incredible geographic potential hostage.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:15.240
It works because most of us don’t&nbsp;
know or think about the Jones Act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.600
We never see its costs directly.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:24.060
It thrives, like many bad&nbsp;
policies, on our lack of awareness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:29.880
